# Lesson 6, batch 2 (2026-11-28): fourth best from AQxxx, underleading the ace at no-trump. Makes.
title: Underleading an ace at no-trump
id: L06-20
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.5, 6.1
student: S
board: 20

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  QT942  832    Q97     J8
  E  A5     AKQ5   J652    KT4
  S  86     J94    T83     AQ752
  W  KJ73   T76    AK4     963

auction:
  W Pass   = Not enough to open
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 2C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 2H     = Four hearts
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 3NT    = Game values, four spades, not four hearts
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E Pass   = No four spades: 3NT it is
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1NT. West's Stayman 2♣ found four hearts with East, but West
  has four spades, not hearts, so the contract is 3NT. East is declarer, and
  so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 15-17 = East's 1NT showed 15 to 17, and 2♥ showed four hearts.
  dummy: 10-14 = West bid game opposite 1NT: 10 to 14, with four spades. (West passed at first, so 12 at most is likely.)
  partner: 2-8 = 40, minus your 7, East's 15 to 17 and West's 10 to 14, leaves 2 to 8.
  remind: The opponents have shown both majors. Which suit is your longest and strongest?

play:
  1   S C5*  W C3  N CJ  E CK
      C5 key: Fourth from your longest and strongest: count down A, Q, 7, 5. Underleading an ace is fine against no-trump: you are setting up the suit.
      C5 not CA: Right suit! But the ace is your way back in later, once the suit is set up. Lead low and keep the ace.
      C5 not CQ: Right suit! The usual card is the fourth from the top: A, Q, 7, 5.
      C5 not C: Right suit! The usual card is the fourth from the top: A, Q, 7, 5.
      C5 not S: West has four spades. Which suit is your longest and strongest?
      C5 not H: Declarer has four hearts. Which suit is your longest and strongest?
      C5 not D: A diamond is a fair unbid suit, but your clubs are longer and stronger.
      note: Partner's jack forces out declarer's king.
  2   E HA  S H4  W H6  N H2
      note: Declarer has nine tricks: four hearts, two spades, two diamonds and the club he just won. He cashes them.
  3   E HK  S H9  W H7  N H3
  4   E HQ  S HJ  W HT  N H8
      note: Your ♥J falls, and declarer's ♥5 is good.
  5   E H5  S D3  W S3  N S2
      note: You keep your clubs and throw a diamond.
  6   E S5  S S6  W SK  N S4
  7   W S7  N S9  E SA  S S8
  8   E D2  S D8  W DA  N D7
  9   W DK  N D9  E D5  S DT
      note: Declarer's ninth trick. He gives up the rest.
  10  W D4  N DQ  E D6  S C2
      note: Partner wins the ♦Q and leads a club through declarer.
  11  N C8  E C4  S CQ  W C6
      note: Now your clubs take the rest.
  12  S CA  W C9  N ST  E CT
  13  S C7  W SJ  N SQ  E DJ

play recap:
  Declarer had nine tricks once your club lead had knocked out his king, so
  3NT made. No lead beats it. But your ♣5 held declarer to nine: a spade,
  heart or diamond lead gives him time to set up a tenth trick before you
  get your clubs going. Leading away from an ace is fine against no-trump,
  and fourth from your longest and strongest is the usual start.
